WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.850
It is great to have back with us today. Rob
Larson, who's a professor of economics and

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.299
also author of the book,

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:13.041
the political economy of Silicon Valley. Uh,
Rob, great to have you on. Thanks David. Great

00:00:13.041 --> 00:00:17.570
to be here. So when I was on the Joe Rogan
program a few months ago, we had this exchange

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:23.960
where he started talking about how of course
a company like Google is a liberal company.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.250
And it was an interesting conversation because
it seemed that what he was talking about was,

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:33.980
you know, does Google have a policy about
anti-trans bullying and that type of thing.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:40.660
And I pointed out, listen, Google is a company
that is advocating for less regulation, is

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:48.649
advocating for at a financial level against
some of the, uh, antitrust, uh, um, uh, interests

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:53.399
that the people may have and may want to have
exercised through their government. And so

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:58.059
if they, you know, have an internal policy
where you can't miss gender people, that's

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:03.930
not really making them a liberal company.
When you consider the sort of broader circumstances

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:08.590
of where they are in the world of regulation
and not paying taxes and so on and so forth,

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:14.189
what is the right way that we should sort
of think about the political orientation of

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:19.939
these big tech companies that often have a
sort of progressive ish seeming public side.

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:24.040
But behind the scenes are really very similar
to, you know, an oil company or whatever else

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.810
the case may be.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:30.920
Yeah, right on. Well that's, you know, very
true. These companies definitely do affect

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:37.429
a very, you know, woke very pro diversity
sort of public posture. But to me, I think

00:01:37.429 --> 00:01:40.740
based on my research there, what right way
to sort of frame this issue is to look at

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.079
it from the point of view of what the company
wants and then what like the workforce wants.

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:49.329
And if you sort of dig into it a lot of the
time when people talk about how these companies

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:54.170
are liberal and they're so on the left, it
sounds goofy because these are after all publicly

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:59.439
traded corporations who stock is held by the
wealthier households of our society and big

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:04.029
financial institutions. How could they be
leftists and anti-capitalist? I think a lot

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:09.300
of the confusion is people look at what the
workforce is saying and then impute that to

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.300
the company.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.640
Like, well, the company must be liberal then.
But of course, as we've seen just in the last

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.470
several months, we've had a couple of fairly
significant labor actions against Google where

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:24.390
the workforce, various components of its enormous
workforce are doing walk-outs and going on

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:30.060
small strikes and doing independent organizing.
And just lately, I mean, I think maybe the

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.940
ultimate, uh, rebuttal to claims these companies
are liberal. It's just lately Google has been

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:41.370
retaining the same classic union busting,
you know, anti labor organization, law firms

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:46.231
that Walmart and Microsoft have been hiring
for years. You know, and especially too, when

00:02:46.231 --> 00:02:51.130
you look at the interface with public policy
in the face of all these new investigations

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:56.090
that big tech, including Google, are subject
to what they call the tech lash. You know,

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:01.540
so the congressional investigations, the,
uh, FTC and justice department, uh, probes

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:06.740
and the state attorneys general, uh, as well,
Google is now one of the biggest lobbying

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:10.970
firms in Washington and spending many million
a year more than all of its tech peers, more

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.940
than Amazon even, uh, on lobbying the government.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.040
And of course they play a big role in election
financing both directly and through their

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.480
packs. So to me, what I would just suggest
to folks like mr Rogan is it's really worth

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.470
taking a small amount of time to differentiate
between what the labor force is pushing for

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:32.930
and what the company wants. And then yes,
like policies, let's say, you know, for example,

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:38.010
oriented for a richer representative staff
as in like perfect, taking the rights of trans

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:44.080
workers versus like the real money aspect
of the equation, which the firms are most

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.319
interested in all the lobbying and maintenance
of their monopoly. Google got the FTC to just

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:53.420
stop investigating, investigating it during
the Obama years. Like that's where these bodies

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.590
are varied. One of the, um,

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.860
big differences between tech companies and
a lot of other companies is that there's often

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:06.250
some kind of, almost like a fairy tale, a
romanticized story about the origins of the

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:12.130
company. You know, Mark Zuckerberg invented
it in, in his, in his Harvard dorm room, invented

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:17.349
Facebook. Jeff Bezos was in some job he hated
and you know, had the idea to start Amazon

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:21.310
and whatever else. You don't see that with
a lot of other types of companies that many

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:26.439
of us interact with regularly, whether they
be car companies of, you know, airline companies

00:04:26.439 --> 00:04:31.039
or whatever the case may be. I'm curious whether
part of that is because a lot of those other

00:04:31.039 --> 00:04:35.509
companies have just been around much longer.
So the story of their creation seems less

00:04:35.509 --> 00:04:40.490
relevant because it's further away or is there
something more, has there been a concerted

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:45.990
effort to keep the mystique through these
sort of fairy tale origin stories?

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:51.770
Yeah, uh, you know, that's a great question.
I think, yeah, pretty much those are the two

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:57.150
dynamics. If you look at all of our great
blue chip fortune 500 companies, including

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:03.779
your classic old accompany giants, you know,
GM and so on, uh, or Ford, uh, you know, IBM,

00:05:03.779 --> 00:05:08.810
they all have this very folksy, yes, small
business. I developed this company in my mom's

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:14.220
basement in the depression sort of origin
mystique. It's true. Uh, these companies are

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.199
all much younger. Of course. Uh, you know,
several of them are only, you know, 10, 20

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:23.629
years old. So their origin story is more recent
and therefore more foregrounded. But also,

00:05:23.629 --> 00:05:27.330
uh, just because of the information based
nature of these products, there's a little

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:32.030
bit more of a, but how did you get the great
idea for this company? Because since they're

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.900
information based, the idea for their platforms
and for their services is kind of more of

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.550
the core of the whole business than it is
if you're a manufacturing light bulbs and

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:41.550
stuff.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.920
So it's true. Like there really is that small
business started in a garage mystique. But

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.529
of course, as I look at in one of the chapters
of a book, uh, the firms vary of course, but

00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:55.749
very typically their origins in terms of their
technology comes straight from the public

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:00.719
sector. So Google again is a classic case.
People talk about, you know, Sarah J and pay

00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:04.889
SRJ brand and Larry Page, they had the idea
to start the firm and it's this great idea

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:08.979
and they built all these great businesses
since I was just wanting to remind people,

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:16.009
Google's original web address wasn't google.com
it was google.stanford.edu because they develop

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:20.009
that at, you know, Stanford, uh, you know,
research labs in their, a computer science

00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:24.949
program, one of the great publicly funded
research university campuses in the United

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:30.099
States where you've got stable funding and
you are allowed to indulge your intellectual

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:34.449
curiosity and think freely about what would
make a good search engine or what would make

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:37.460
a good, you know, social networking system
in the marketplace.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:42.809
You're under a near term pressure for profitability
to get your stock price up and keep wall street

00:06:42.809 --> 00:06:46.969
happy with you in the academic world. You're
free to take your time. And of course the

00:06:46.969 --> 00:06:51.379
internet itself, after all was originally
developed by a DARPA, you know, the Pentagon's

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:56.770
research arm along with the university system.
And in the book I just go through, you know,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:00.199
Amazon, Facebook, Google and the internet
and the hardware itself, like the stuff in

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:05.369
your smartphone, you know, almost all of that
technology comes from one or another public

00:07:05.369 --> 00:07:10.629
sector research institution or at least through
heavy public funding, which again insulates

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:14.400
you from that short term market pressure.
So to me, the story of the origins of these

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.819
firms is one of the juiciest phony aspects
about them will. So to stick with that a little

00:07:18.819 --> 00:07:19.819
bit. Yeah.

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:24.009
Can you talk a little bit about what sort
of regulatory or financial incentives also

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:28.499
existed that allowed these companies to do
what they did and to grow, uh, as quickly

00:07:28.499 --> 00:07:32.800
as they did? I mean, it's certainly at a base
level, people increasingly dependent on the

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.120
internet and more and more of day to day society
being run through the internet is a huge boon

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.400
to accompany that exists on mobile phones
and on the internet for sure. But in terms

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:47.960
of regulation, tax breaks, etc. What else
was done that allowed these companies to grow

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:49.900
so quickly at the start?

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:57.449
For sure. Well, the very, um, loose regulatory
framework is an important part of this story.

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:01.879
Uh, all through the era when the internet
and internet based services were becoming

00:08:01.879 --> 00:08:06.129
more popularized like in the, I'm old enough
to remember in the 90s, uh, when the internet

00:08:06.129 --> 00:08:11.300
came to town and you could go to some library
campuses and look up other card catalogs,

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:19.099
uh, like th at that time it was a bipartisan
um, view that what you don't want to do is

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:23.680
regulate the internet or online industries
cause that's going to impose new costs on

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.550
them or limit their, you know, their innovation
and their creativity. And for the longest

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.590
time, like, well through the Bush years into,
into the Obama years, that was something that

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:36.770
was more or less bipartisan. We don't want
to regulate the net. It's growing. It's giving

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.520
us all these fun free services and so on.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.900
Now of course, as these firms have become
so important, and as you say, it's, we're

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:48.810
so reliant on them and we start seeing their
power in elections in our society, in the

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:52.660
way that we do with everything through our
phones. People are a lot more interested in

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:57.660
putting some leashes on their behavior and
on their power. But to me the real issue with

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:03.250
having had such a loose regulatory framework
for so long for big tech is what I talk about

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:08.120
in the first chapter of the book. These are
markets that are subject to what we call network

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:13.300
effects in economics and network effect is
just something that applies to certain markets

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:18.100
where an information based product is at play.
And the big thing there is that, uh, with

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:24.150
those sorts of markets, the service gains
value, the more people use it. So like a telephone,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:28.160
you know, it's more people get on the phone
network, your telephone gets a little bit

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.810
more valuable to you because there's more
and more people you can potentially call.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:37.110
Well, the same network effects apply to companies
like Facebook. You want to be on the network

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:41.350
that has all your friends on it, not on the
one that has no one on it. And Google search,

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:46.620
even it's more people to do Google searches
day in and day out. There are algorithms keep

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:51.440
learning from that. And the more search searches
that are done, the stronger their algorithm

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.880
and search processes become. So the thing
about network effects, is there a strong incentive

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:03.310
that leads to monopolization or maybe having
two or three very dominant firms in a market.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:08.740
And that is what we see in big tech from Facebook's
and Instagram's, you know, oligopoly to, you

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.900
know, Google's mobile search, monopoly to
YouTube. There's a long list, but it's all

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.500
these straight economic aspects to it. So

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:21.160
there was a big deal made when Amazon, basically
after being shamed by Bernie Sanders, decided

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.650
that they were going to start paying everybody
15 bucks an hour. And the end result, I thought

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:29.850
was great. Of course, it was a calculation
for Amazon that there was going to be a PR

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:34.090
benefit, more than likely that in the long
term would exceed the additional cost to them

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:38.530
of paying the 15 bucks an hour. But let's
ignore that for a second. Let's imagine that

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:43.940
it worked in that an elected official shamed
a company and they made the right decision

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:49.590
when Donald Trump, between being elected and
inaugurated, went to, I believe it was Indiana

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:56.420
or Iowa, Indiana, and announced that he struck
a deal with carrier air conditioner, that

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:01.380
they were going to keep their factory open
in Indiana and what he basically did was just

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:05.340
give him money, right? There was a plan where
carrier decided we're leaving and Trump came

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.140
in and said, Hey, here's some money if you
stay.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:12.080
My criticism at the time was this is not a
scalable economic policy. This is saying,

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:16.530
Hey, I found a company that has already made
a business decision. I'm changing the calculus

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:21.590
by giving them some cash, and of course in
the end that money was used to start automating

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:26.791
away those jobs. Anyway, half of the workforce
still went to Mexico. It was a disaster. There's

00:11:26.791 --> 00:11:33.690
not that much of a difference in a sense between
Trump giving money to carrier to get them

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:39.750
to stay and Bernie pressuring one company
to pay 15 bucks an hour. Now, of course the,

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:44.210
the one that Bernie did is working out much
better in terms of his result. It's results,

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:49.440
but my question is about the scalability of
policy when it comes to these things. We can't

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:54.830
really be relying on elected officials to
be making one off deals, can we, I mean, it

00:11:54.830 --> 00:12:01.370
seems to me that we need something systemic
to be done about regulation, incentives, job

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.490
creation, whatever the case may be.

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:11.740
Oh, absolutely. And I think even like across
the wide spectrum of opinions about big tech,

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:17.000
I think that that is sort of recognized. So
again, whether you're suspicious of a big

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.550
tech firm, a more laughed or a more conservative
point of view, I think broadly it's recognized

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:27.860
what you need. Yeah. As a set of stable rules
that are uniform one company to another. Yeah.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:32.100
And stable across time. You can't just be
piecemeal, threatening or throwing cash at

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.730
these firms. But again, what it sort of comes
back to though is the power of these firms,

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:42.820
like most companies couldn't get all of America's
cities to debase themselves and beg for a

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:48.320
second headquarters. But Amazon absolutely
was able to do that. And it was quite a spectacle.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.890
And they ended up splitting the investment
and walking away from New York. And then of

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:56.720
course putting tens of thousands of jobs there
anyway without millions of dollars in cash,

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:04.240
which kind of shows perhaps the lack of necessity
for these huge subsidy and incentive packages.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.570
But hopefully with these new investigations
we have coming down at the national level

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:12.680
and indeed the international level of these
big tech firms. You know, we do have the FTC

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:17.410
and the justice department here and overseas,
like the strongest regulations so far that

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:22.980
any of these firms are subject to is all emanating
from the European union, from privacy to data

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.691
storage to the right to be forgotten. It's
interesting and of course I talk about it

00:13:26.691 --> 00:13:31.550
in the book a bit, um, cause the difference
in these regulatory conditions, plus it does

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:35.860
end up mattering that all of these big tech
platform companies and their monopolies are

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:41.720
semi monopolies. They are all American. And
the European union is used to being partially

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.380
dominated by American firms. You know, from
IBM to Coke. But they're a little more willing

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:51.370
to regulate firms that are out of state. Um,
despite, you know, a real political presence

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:53.310
that those firms have overseas too.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:57.720
But I think a new rules based approach is
very reasonable, especially one that limits

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:03.490
the power of these firms. Again, we have a
fairly long record now of all of these firms.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:08.370
Once network effects builds up their market
power from Microsoft all the way to Google

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:13.580
and Facebook, they aren't shy about using
it to crush competitors and destroy rivals.

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:17.730
I mentioned this in the book, there's just
a piece of wall street journal reporting about

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:21.900
Facebook and they didn't seem to be using
this word ironically, but they mentioned that

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:26.900
Facebook has a whole little division in the
company whose job is to monitor startups.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:32.230
You know, new, small web-based firms that
might potentially compete with Facebook or

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:37.220
one of its other apps like Instagram or messenger.
And they try to buy up that from at a very

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.770
early stage when they can buy it for just
a million bucks from some founder in their

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:44.890
garage or crush it or go after it legally
in some other way.

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:50.280
And the way the journal described it was Facebook
is out to disrupt any potential challengers,

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:53.850
which is kind of funny because of course all
these firms talk about is how we're disrupters

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:58.530
and we're breaking up that inefficient old
economy now that they're the economy and the

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:02.520
five biggest companies in the U S are all
these tech platforms now, which is unheard

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:08.190
of. They try to disrupt and you know, put
control over their upstarts and small competitors

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:13.140
too. So I think rules that reign in these
firms power or even go further and putting

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:18.090
the workforce and more in charge or you know,
and in that way potentially partially socializing

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:21.410
the platforms. To me, those are the things
we should look at. Things that address the

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:25.030
firm's market power. No question about that.
We've been

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:30.410
speaking with Rob Larson, who's a professor
of economics of the book is called [inaudible]

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:35.150
tyrants, the political economy of Silicon
Valley. We are linking to the book. Definitely

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:38.880
check it out Rob. Always a pleasure having
you on next day. My pleasure

